# Port Plan — Step 10: A Standard Tool Library (Ruby → Python)

Port the tool library — filesystem tools, shell tools, and MUD gameplay tools —
plus the config, wiring and REPL changes that carry them.

This is the tenth of thirteen steps. **Step 09 is skipped** (see below), so this
is the first step whose Python folder is a copy of a non-adjacent Ruby step.

> **The headline: the largest file in Ruby step 10 must not be ported.**
> `tools/mud.rb` is 480 lines of MUD gameplay tools built directly on the
> `mud_manager` gem. Python has no such gem and is not getting one. It obtains
> **the same 26 tools** by spawning `mud-manager --mcp` and asking it what it
> has. That is not a shortcut around the port — it is the entire reason the MCP
> daemon was built (`generic_interfacing` §7: *"Python / Go / Rust / Java: point
> the framework's MCP client at `mud-manager --mcp`. Zero MUD/telnet code."*).
> This step is where that claim gets tested by a second language for the first
> time.

